(a) Circular Queue implementation:

void push(int n) {
    // Check if queue is full
    if ((rear + 1) % cap == front) {
        System.out.println("QUEUE IS FULL");
        return;
    }
    // If queue is empty
    if (front == 0 && rear == 0) {
        front = 1;
        rear = 1;
    } else {
        rear = (rear % cap) + 1; // circular increment
    }
    cq[rear] = n;
}

int pop() {
    if (front == 0) return -9999; // queue empty
    int val = cq[front];
    if (front == rear) {
        // Last element removed
        front = 0;
        rear = 0;
    } else {
        front = (front % cap) + 1; // circular increment
    }
    return val;
}

(b) Difference:
Linear queue: When elements are deleted from front, that space cannot be reused (false overflow). Rear pointer only moves forward.
Circular queue: The rear pointer wraps around to the beginning when it reaches the end, reusing freed space. Avoids false overflow. More memory-efficient.

